{
	"version": 5,
	"id": "B-InkK53lOmsXJ0Vh",
	"notes": "An add-on, not a full character: layer this over Imperium/Templates/ Astartes Battle-Brother.gct plus the Marine's own Chapter Gene-Seed lens from Astartes Gene-Seed.adq (keep that lens — a Deathwatch posting changes nothing about a Marine's physiology or Chapter of origin, and a seconded Marine's gene-seed quirks, Chapter Rank and Chapter-side Duty are all still in force underneath the traits below). Swap the Battle-Brother's own Boltgun, Godwyn Pattern and Bolt Pistol, Mk II Phobos-Pattern for whichever of this add-on's own Deathwatch Wargear.eqp weapons the kill-team's mission calls for; keep the Battle-Brother's chainsword, combat knife and grenades unless the player wants the Xenophase Blade instead of a chainsword.\nThis add-on's own traits and skills, at their default (rank-and-file, no optional lens enabled) settings, come to 80 points — see the point math below. The Astartes Battle-Brother.gct template it layers onto totals 821 points before equipment (Astartes package with ST 30 and DX 16, plus training and skills), so a rank-and-file Deathwatch Marine is about 901. Enabling any of the disabled lenses below (Watch Sergeant, Watch Master, Black Shield, Veteran, extra Deathwatch Authority) adds further points on top of that, per each lens's own note.\nPoint math for this add-on's default build: -10 (Duty: The Long Watch) - 1 (Vigil Oath quirk) - 5 (Sense of Duty, Kill-team, included from Imperial Traits.adq) - 1 (Chapter Rivalry quirk) = -17 of disadvantage; + 40 (Xenos Hunter Talent 4 at 10/level) + 5 (Xenos Bane: Tyranids, enabled by default as the archetypal Deathwatch specialisation — swap which child is enabled for a different xenos focus) = 45 of advantage; + 52 of skills (Hidden Lore (Xenos) +8, Tactics +8, Survival (Any) +8, Tracking +8, Gesture +4, Soldier +8, and a new Guns (Shotgun) 8, all additive to the Battle-Brother's own entries of the same name where one already exists, per each skill line's own note) = -17 + 45 + 52 = 80. The disabled Watch Sergeant, Watch Master, Black Shield, Veteran and extra Deathwatch Authority lenses below are not part of this baseline; each states what enabling it costs on top.\n",
	"traits": [
		{
			"id": "tRTApox_z3u8AUlwZ",
			"name": "Deathwatch Oath: The Long Watch",
			"reference": "B133",
			"local_notes": "Quite Often, Hazardous. Covers only the Battle-Brother's secondment to the Deathwatch itself; it is layered on top of (not a replacement for) his own Chapter's Duty (Chapter and Imperium), which the Astartes Hypno-Indoctrination package already prices. A seconded Marine answers to two chains of command until his tour ends.\n\nLore: Chapters (bar a handful of holdouts and the notorious exceptions the Inquisition has learned to work around) send the Deathwatch Battle-Brothers under the ancient Pact of the Long Vigil: the Watch was founded under the direction of the Inquisition's Ordo Xenos to hunt the alien, and the Chapters contribute Marines seconded to it. A seconded Marine takes a fresh oath at the Watch Fortress he reports to, serves a term of the Chapter Master's choosing (often years, sometimes a Marine's remaining service), and lives, trains and fights alongside strangers from other Chapters for its duration — Black Library's Deathwatch-focused fiction (notably Steve Parker's Deathwatch novel and the Deathwatch anthologies) and Codex: Deathwatch background both treat the tour's end as a real, if sometimes delayed by circumstance, event: a Marine can and does go home.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Duty (B133) at a lighter frequency and cost than the Chapter Duty it sits alongside (Quite Often rather than Almost All the Time, -10 rather than the Chapter Duty's -20): the danger is not less real, but the obligation is bounded to a term rather than lifelong, and unlike Chapter Duty it does not also carry the weight of \"and Imperium\" — that half is still covered by the Marine's own Chapter oath. This project deliberately does not fold Deathwatch service into a single bigger Duty trait; keeping the two separate lets a GM show a character's tour ending (drop this trait, keep the Chapter one) without touching the racial package.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Astartes",
				"Deathwatch"
			],
			"base_points": -10
		},
		{
			"id": "tgL1a5OyTZ3mHJDFd",
			"name": "Vigil Oath (Silence of the Long Watch)",
			"reference": "B22",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Every kill-team brother swears never to speak, outside the Watch Fortress, of what he learns there — xenobiology, captured wargear, Ordo Xenos intelligence, the failures as well as the victories. A recurring beat in Deathwatch fiction is a Marine's visible discomfort when a well-meaning ally or debriefing officer pushes for details he has sworn not to give.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Quirk (B22): a specific, roleplayable behavioural restriction rather than a fresh Vow (which would be redundant with the Duty trait above and the Chapter's existing Code of Honor); priced at the flat -1 every quirk in this project uses.",
			"tags": [
				"Quirk",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Astartes",
				"Deathwatch"
			],
			"base_points": -1
		},
		{
			"id": "t0qqB0VrFxW2y1ZHu",
			"name": "Sense of Duty (Squadmates)",
			"reference": "B153",
			"local_notes": "The kill-team, not the squad — see Deathwatch.adq's own copy of this entry for the full note; referenced directly from Imperial Traits.adq here rather than chained through Deathwatch.adq, since this project's build tool does not resolve an include-of-an-include.\n\nLore: Small combat units live and die by cohesion; abandoning a wounded comrade is the one thing that reliably gets a soldier shunned by their own side, per how Guard squads and Sororitas/Militarum Tempestus kill-teams are consistently written in Black Library fiction.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Sense of Duty (B153), small-group scope, -5.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": -5
		},
		{
			"id": "tURPwWiyg30JYkaiy",
			"name": "Chapter Rivalry",
			"reference": "B22",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Centuries of doctrinal friction don't evaporate because two Marines now share a kill-team badge: a White Scar's impatience with a Dark Angel's secrecy, an Imperial Fist's dim view of a Space Wolf's informality, old grudges between Successor lines. Deathwatch fiction treats this as background texture the kill-team works through or around, not a fight that breaks the squad — Watch Sergeants exist precisely to keep it that way.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Quirk (B22): a specific, GM-adjudicated friction with one other Chapter (player's or GM's choice) that never rises to a combat-relevant penalty, distinct from the Sense of Duty above (which is what actually governs whether the character risks himself for the squad) and from any one Chapter's own gene-seed quirks in Astartes Gene-Seed.adq, which are unaffected.",
			"tags": [
				"Quirk",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Astartes",
				"Deathwatch"
			],
			"base_points": -1
		},
		{
			"id": "tzU6cjdu1T0c8CLPJ",
			"name": "Xenos Hunter",
			"reference": "B89",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Years spent studying, hunting and killing a rotating cast of alien threats give a Deathwatch veteran a working, cross-species battlefield expertise no single-Chapter Astartes accumulates: reading an unfamiliar xenos' tactics fast enough to counter them, tracking quarry across terrain built for another biology, and pattern-matching captured wargear or physiology against Ordo Xenos archives from memory. This is the throughline of Deathwatch's Kill Team-focused fiction and Codex: Deathwatch background alike: the kill-team's edge is knowing the enemy, not just outgunning it.\n\nDesign: Built as a Basic Set Talent (B89-90): a single leveled trait giving a flat bonus to a cluster of related skills (Hidden Lore (Xenos), Tactics, Survival, Tracking — four skills, priced at the 10 points/level GURPS uses for a Talent covering three to five skills, B89; the Psyker Talent in Psykers and the Warp.adq uses the same rate) plus a modest reaction bonus from people who share or respect the specialty, per B90's Talent template. This is the \"Talent\" the framework's checklist asks for, honestly built rather than reusing Sorcery Talent or any other book Talent under a new name.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium",
				"Deathwatch",
				"Talent"
			],
			"points_per_level": 10,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 4,
