		{
			"id": "TF2njHY5SwrkunVgy",
			"name": "Watch Sergeant",
			"local_notes": "Disabled lens for a Marine who leads his own kill-team. Enable for +10 points (Deathwatch Rank 2).\n\nLore: A Battle-Brother trusted to lead a mixed-Chapter kill-team in the field answers to the Watch Fortress as a Watch Sergeant — the most common command rank an active Deathwatch Marine actually holds, per Codex: Deathwatch and the wider Deathwatch RPG line's own kill-team structure.\n\nDesign: Deathwatch Rank at level 2 (10 points), reusing the leveled trait from Deathwatch.adq rather than restating Basic Set Rank math here.",
			"tags": [
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Astartes",
				"Deathwatch"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "td4VQAqkHGXlpKMOc",
					"name": "Deathwatch Rank",
					"reference": "B29",
					"local_notes": "Levels: 2 Watch Sergeant (leads one kill-team), 4 Watch Captain (commands a Watch Fortress's worth of kill-teams and answers directly to that fortress's Ordo Xenos liaison), 6 Watch Master (head of the entire Deathwatch, one of the very few Astartes officers who reports to the Inquisition, in practice, as something closer to a peer than a subordinate). Levels 1, 3 and 5 cover intermediate seniority with no distinct title in the sources consulted. This Rank governs standing within the Deathwatch's own chain of command only; it neither replaces nor outranks a Marine's Chapter Rank back home.\n\nLore: The Deathwatch runs a rank structure parallel to, and largely independent of, every founding Chapter's own: a seconded Battle-Brother answers first to his kill-team's Watch Sergeant, Watch Sergeants to a Watch Fortress's Watch Captain, and Watch Captains (however many the Deathwatch currently fields) to a single Watch Master, per Codex: Deathwatch background and its consistent portrayal across Black Library's Deathwatch line. A Watch Master's authority to requisition matériel and troops for a xenos-hunting mission is, in practice, backed by the Inquisition's own Ordo Xenos.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Rank (B29), 5 points/level, built the same way as this project's existing Military Rank and Religious Rank entries (Imperial Traits.adq) rather than inventing a new mechanic: one leveled trait covers the whole ladder, and the notes field does the work of mapping levels to the named offices the framework asks for (Watch Master and Watch Captain) instead of splitting them into separate un-leveled traits that would have to be kept in sync by hand.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Social",
						"Imperium",
						"Deathwatch"
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 2
				}
			],
			"disabled": true
		},
		{
			"id": "TOCEUfJJUmdyJbNj1",
			"name": "Watch Master",
			"local_notes": "Disabled lens for the single Astartes who commands the entire Deathwatch. Enable for +30 points (Deathwatch Rank 6) plus, at a GM's discretion, several levels of Deathwatch Authority above this add-on's own baseline — a Watch Master's writ reaches further than a single kill-team's. Firmly a \"Heroes and legends\" character once enabled, not a rank-and-file kill-team build; see framework.md's point-scale table.\n\nLore: The Deathwatch's single overall commander, one of the very few Astartes officers who deals with the Inquisition as something close to a peer rather than a subordinate; see Deathwatch.adq's own Rank entry for the sourcing.\n\nDesign: Deathwatch Rank at level 6 (30 points), reused from Deathwatch.adq.",
			"tags": [
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Astartes",
				"Deathwatch"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "t-1ksYtSmU9OUbBpz",
					"name": "Deathwatch Rank",
					"reference": "B29",
					"local_notes": "Levels: 2 Watch Sergeant (leads one kill-team), 4 Watch Captain (commands a Watch Fortress's worth of kill-teams and answers directly to that fortress's Ordo Xenos liaison), 6 Watch Master (head of the entire Deathwatch, one of the very few Astartes officers who reports to the Inquisition, in practice, as something closer to a peer than a subordinate). Levels 1, 3 and 5 cover intermediate seniority with no distinct title in the sources consulted. This Rank governs standing within the Deathwatch's own chain of command only; it neither replaces nor outranks a Marine's Chapter Rank back home.\n\nLore: The Deathwatch runs a rank structure parallel to, and largely independent of, every founding Chapter's own: a seconded Battle-Brother answers first to his kill-team's Watch Sergeant, Watch Sergeants to a Watch Fortress's Watch Captain, and Watch Captains (however many the Deathwatch currently fields) to a single Watch Master, per Codex: Deathwatch background and its consistent portrayal across Black Library's Deathwatch line. A Watch Master's authority to requisition matériel and troops for a xenos-hunting mission is, in practice, backed by the Inquisition's own Ordo Xenos.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Rank (B29), 5 points/level, built the same way as this project's existing Military Rank and Religious Rank entries (Imperial Traits.adq) rather than inventing a new mechanic: one leveled trait covers the whole ladder, and the notes field does the work of mapping levels to the named offices the framework asks for (Watch Master and Watch Captain) instead of splitting them into separate un-leveled traits that would have to be kept in sync by hand.",
