				}
			],
			"disabled": true
		},
		{
			"id": "TWb-mA7j6CDWBVcIr",
			"name": "Black Shield",
			"local_notes": "Disabled lens for a Marine who serves with no Chapter badge. Enable for -11 points (Black Shield, Deathwatch.adq) and, at the table's discretion, disable/replace the Marine's own Chapter Gene-Seed lens with the plain Astartes ancestry trait if the character's true Chapter is meant to be unknown even to the player — this project leaves that choice to the GM rather than assuming it, since Black Shields in the fiction sometimes do know their own origin even when no one else does.\n\nLore: See Deathwatch.adq's own Black Shield entry for full sourcing.\n\nDesign: Thin wrapper so this template can list Black Shield as a lens alongside the Rank lenses, matching the Chapter Gene-Seed lenses' own container pattern in Astartes Gene-Seed.adq.",
			"tags": [
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Astartes",
				"Deathwatch",
				"Chapter Variant"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "Tod57f-tKt4scCqJK",
					"name": "Black Shield",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A minority of Deathwatch Marines serve with no Chapter badge at all beneath the Deathwatch cross — \"Black Shields\" whose origin is known, if at all, only to the Watch Master who accepted their oath. Black Library's Deathwatch fiction treats the reasons as varied and often left unresolved on the page: a Chapter destroyed to the last man, a Marine whose Chapter disowned him, an Inquisitorial placement too sensitive to badge, or simply personal choice. What's consistent across sources is that other Astartes find a badgeless brother unsettling, and the Deathwatch itself is one of the very few institutions in the Imperium that will take such a Marine at all.\n\nDesign: A meta-trait lens exactly like the Chapter Gene-Seed lenses in Astartes Gene-Seed.adq, but for concealed origin rather than physiology: Secret (B152) at a moderate cost since what's actually at risk varies by the individual's backstory (a GM ruling this session's specific Black Shield's secret is more dangerous should raise the cost accordingly, per B152's own guidance), plus a 1-point Quirk flagging the in-fiction \"other Marines find this unsettling\" beat as a roleplaying hook rather than a mechanical reaction penalty (which would double-count against the Sense of Duty and Chapter Rivalry quirks above).",
					"tags": [
						"Imperium",
						"Adeptus Astartes",
						"Deathwatch",
						"Chapter Variant"
					],
					"container_type": "meta_trait",
					"children": [
						{
							"id": "tf9A3lKRhOsBvqmPC",
							"name": "Secret (True Chapter of Origin)",
							"reference": "B152",
							"local_notes": "Moderate exposure: discovery risks anything from Chapter reprisal to an Inquisitorial recall, depending on why the Marine left his Chapter unnamed in the first place — left deliberately vague, per the fiction's own refusal to give a single answer.",
							"base_points": -10
						},
						{
							"id": "touOWsM_ZoLk4hmf3",
							"name": "No Chapter Badge",
							"local_notes": "Wears plain, unmarked black plate with no Chapter iconography beneath the Deathwatch's own heraldry; instantly recognisable as a Black Shield to anyone who knows the sign, and quietly distrusted by the superstitious for it.",
							"tags": [
								"Quirk"
							],
							"base_points": -1
						}
					]
				}
			],
			"disabled": true
		},
		{
			"id": "T0h2AdipWjn1cpCf5",
			"name": "Veteran (Kill-team Specialist)",
			"local_notes": "Disabled lens for a Vanguard-tier or otherwise especially experienced kill-team veteran. Enable for +25 points, all of it free skill points (this lens intentionally adds skill points rather than new combat advantages, since the Astartes and Hypno-Indoctrination packages already cover reflexes and pain tolerance).\n\nLore: Deathwatch fiction (the Deathwatch anthologies in particular) consistently shows its named Vanguard-tier veterans as the ones a Watch Captain assigns to the hardest postings, not because they hit harder in an absolute sense but because they've simply done this longer.\n\nDesign: A flat skill-point lens rather than a new combat trait, per the note above.",
			"tags": [
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Astartes",
				"Deathwatch"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tNjHT0M_ROS7aArzM",
					"name": "Veteran Skill Package",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A Marine who has run enough kill-team postings picks up a second specialisation and sharper all-round battlefield judgement than a first-tour secondee, matching how Deathwatch fiction treats its long-serving Vanguard veterans as noticeably more capable than a newly-arrived Battle-Brother, without making them a different kind of soldier.\n\nDesign: 25 points of free skill-point improvement (GM/player allocates across this add-on's own skills below, or the Battle-Brother's), rather than a second Xenos Hunter Talent level or a new combat trait, so a \"veteran\" build stays a better-trained version of the same Marine instead of quietly becoming a Librarian-tier specialist through the back door.",
					"tags": [
						"Imperium",
						"Deathwatch"
					],
					"base_points": 25
				}
			],
			"disabled": true
		},
		{
			"id": "TExmEJp_-9ikeT4vE",
			"name": "Deathwatch Authority (Ordo Xenos Writ)",
			"local_notes": "Disabled by default (see this file's own point-math note above). Enable at level 1 for +5, level 2 for +10, or the full level 3 for +15 (Deathwatch.adq).\n\nLore: See Deathwatch.adq's own entry for sourcing.\n\nDesign: Thin wrapper, same pattern as the Black Shield lens above.",
			"tags": [
				"Imperium",
				"Deathwatch",
				"Inquisition"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "t413JCz89HW3GUxCY",
					"name": "Deathwatch Authority (Ordo Xenos Writ)",
					"reference": "B65",
					"local_notes": "Scoped to xenos-threat operations specifically: commandeering local Imperial forces and matériel, overriding a planetary commander's order to disengage, and demanding logistical priority from the Departmento Munitorum when a kill-team is in the field. It does not extend to policing the Inquisition's own internal affairs (that remains Inquisitorial Rank in Imperial Traits.adq) and, per the Deathwatch's own founding compact, is never turned against another Chapter's internal discipline.\n\nLore: A Deathwatch kill-team operates under a writ the Inquisition's Ordo Xenos backs to the hilt whenever a confirmed alien threat is in play: Codex: Deathwatch and Black Library's Deathwatch fiction both show planetary governors, Naval officers and even Astra Militarum generals stepping aside for a kill-team's Watch Sergeant once the writ is shown, precisely because the Ordo Xenos has learned that hesitation in the face of a genuine xenos incursion gets worlds lost.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Legal Enforcement Powers (B65), 5/level, at 3 levels (15 points) — the same shape as Imperial Traits.adq's Legal Enforcement Powers (Commissariat), reused as a template rather than invented fresh, but scoped narrower in the notes (xenos operations only) to keep it distinct from an actual Inquisitorial rosette, which this trait deliberately does not grant.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Social",
						"Imperium",
						"Deathwatch",
						"Inquisition"
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 1,
					"max_levels": 3
				}
			],
			"disabled": true
		}
	],
	"skills": [
		{
			"id": "sMlXgTTFTnLVZCser",
			"name": "Hidden Lore",
			"reference": "B199",
			"local_notes": "Add these 8 points to the Battle-Brother's own Hidden Lore (Xenos) 8 points (already on that template, for 16 total) rather than tracking a second entry — a seconded Marine's xenos-lore training deepens what he already studied at the Chapter, it doesn't start over.\n\nLore: What the Inquisition and the more paranoid chaplains know of daemonic nature, names, and countermeasures: pict-evidence, interrogation transcripts and forbidden grimoires rather than fieldcraft. Widely depicted as dangerous knowledge in the Eisenhorn/Ravenor and Gaunt's Ghosts novels; Dark Heresy/Only War use an equivalent Forbidden Lore (Daemonology) skill for calibration.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Hidden Lore (B199), specialisation per framework.md. Hidden Lore is IQ/Average with no default in the Basic Set, so none is listed here or on the other Hidden Lore specialisations.",
			"tags": [
				"Mental",
				"Knowledge",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"specialization": "Xenos",
			"difficulty": "iq/a",
			"points": 8
		},
		{
			"id": "s2Q0Onkt2TKpoQVOV",
			"name": "Tactics",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Add to the Battle-Brother's own Tactics 24 points (for 32 total): cross-Chapter kill-team doctrine specifically, on top of the single-Chapter combat doctrine the base template already covers.\n\nDesign: 8 points of Tactics improvement, same additive-to-the-base-template pattern the Hidden Lore (Xenos) line above uses, rather than a second stand-alone Tactics entry on the character sheet.",
			"tags": [
				"Imperium",
				"Deathwatch"
			],
			"difficulty": "iq/h",
			"points": 8
		},
		{
			"id": "sShEzydGz70Cx4ozZ",
			"name": "Survival",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Add to the Battle-Brother's own Survival (Any) 8 points (for 16 total): tracking and surviving across whatever terrain a mission demands, on worlds no single Chapter trains for.\n\nDesign: Additive-to-the-base-template pattern, as above.",
			"tags": [
				"Imperium",
				"Deathwatch"
			],
			"specialization": "Any",
			"difficulty": "per/a",
			"points": 8
		},
		{
			"id": "sO_hQuZ4rVRb9dwrg",
			"name": "Tracking",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Add to the Battle-Brother's own Tracking 2 points (for 10 total): following unfamiliar xenos quarry across terrain built for another biology, a routine kill-team task per Deathwatch fiction.\n\nDesign: Additive-to-the-base-template pattern, as above.",
			"tags": [
				"Imperium",
				"Deathwatch"
			],
			"difficulty": "per/a",
			"points": 8
		},
		{
			"id": "sAM516bwRNvatxeUW",
			"name": "Gesture",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Add to the Battle-Brother's own Gesture 1 point (for 5 total): a kill-team drills a shared battle-sign that has to work across Chapters with no common signing tradition, not just within one Chapter's own squads.\n\nDesign: Additive-to-the-base-template pattern, as above.",
			"tags": [
				"Imperium",
				"Deathwatch"
			],
			"difficulty": "iq/e",
			"points": 4
		},
		{
			"id": "s9Dodpi0LuK7SfHp-",
			"name": "Soldier",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Add to the Battle-Brother's own Soldier 12 points (for 20 total): Deathwatch field protocols, Watch Fortress procedure and Ordo Xenos liaison work on top of a Marine's own Chapter soldiering.\n\nDesign: Additive-to-the-base-template pattern, as above.",
			"tags": [
				"Imperium",
				"Deathwatch"
			],
			"difficulty": "iq/a",
			"points": 8
		},
		{
			"id": "s_2_OTyWEPKUSHUsW",
			"name": "Guns",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Deathwatch Marine training on the kill-team's own shotgun and its specialist shells, a weapon type the standard Battle-Brother template doesn't otherwise train.\n\nDesign: New skill line (the base template has no Guns (Shotgun) entry to add to), Easy difficulty per the framework's own weapon-skill table; priced at a full 8 points (not the 1-2 point \"familiarity\" level) since the shotgun is this add-on's own primary breaching weapon, not a rarely-touched sidearm.",
			"tags": [
				"Imperium",
				"Deathwatch"
			],
			"specialization": "Shotgun",
			"difficulty": "dx/e",
			"points": 8
		}
	],
	"equipment": [
		{
			"id": "E5NQwBxGyCg_vrbYI",
			"description": "Power Armour, Deathwatch Livery",
			"local_notes": "Weak Points 4: when a hit on this armour fails to penetrate, roll 3d; on 4 or less it found a gap and DR is halved against it.\n\nReferenced directly from Imperial Armour.eqp rather than chained through Deathwatch Wargear.eqp's own copy of this entry, since this project's build tool does not resolve an include-of-an-include; see Deathwatch Wargear.eqp for the full note on why this is the same suit repainted, not a new one.\n\nLore: Mark VII \"Aquila Armour\" is the standardised post-Heresy pattern issued across the newly re-formed Chapters and remains, per current Codex Space Marines background and countless 41st-millennium Black Library novels, the most commonly depicted Astartes plate to this day.\n\nDesign: Torso DR and eye-lens DR are both taken directly from the fixed anchor (\"Astartes power armour (Mk VII baseline) | 100 ... Eye lenses DR 40\", framework.md); every other Mark in this file is derived relative to this entry. Limb/head DR are torso minus 12 for arms and legs, minus 25 for face and feet, and minus 37 for neck and hands; the Mark II to Mark VI patterns keep these offsets. The Mark VII's own servo bonus (lifting +9, striking +5) is the reference value the other marks scale from. GCS adds a container's children to its own weight and cost, so the shell entry is the plate itself (150 lb, $40,000); with its subsystems (90 lb, $30,000) the assembled suit is 240 lb and $70,000. The servo bonus lives on the Servo-Motor Actuators child only, so it is counted once.",
			"tags": [
				"Body Armor",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Astartes"
			],
			"tech_level": "11",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "40000",
			"base_weight": "150 lb",
